[2026-10-02 18:16:56 EDT]   [Review] What worked: Kingambit lead vs Indeedee logic was sound in principle, and Whimsicott's Moonblast chip plus Garchomp's Stomping Tantrum did eventually remove Incineroar. Drogon's Mega + Weather Ball finally pressured Indeedee, and Whimsicott's Protect on T7 correctly dodged a listed KO threat.
[2026-10-02 18:16:56 EDT]   [Review] What failed: 1) T1 Kingambit attacked into a listed 71-108% OHKO and died immediately — a 1-for-0 trade that handed them the whole game and left Drogon exposed to Zap Cannon. 2) We Encored Milotic into COIL on T2 and T5 — Encore on a boosting move locks the foe into continuing to boost. That is the opposite of punishment. Milotic ended at +6 Atk/+6 Def/+6 accuracy and killed two of our Pokémon with Icy Wind. 3) Garchomp was Choice-locked into Stomping Tantrum and we kept firing it at a target that Protected, then switched; 3 of its actions produced nothing. 4) Only 2 Tailwind turns the whole game; Whimsicott spent its turns on Encore/Moonblast instead of the one move that mattered. 5) Drogon Mega'd on turn 7 — our win condition sat on the bench while Raichu-Y farmed, then got OHKO'd by Zap Cannon at full HP. 6) Damage calcs were off three times (Weather Ball predicted 8-12% actually did 64%), meaning our engine's assumed sets were wrong and our 'this won't KO' reasoning was unreliable.
[2026-10-02 18:16:56 EDT] [Team Inspiration] Recorded suggestion inspired by opponent build: "Copy their 'boost behind a redirecting/bulky partner' plan: Coil Milotic became unkillable because Incineroar then Indeedee (Follow Me) absorbed every action while it boosted six times. For us: give our Milotic Coil-proof pressure by running Ice Beam + Scald with max HP/max SpD Leftovers AND bring Kingambit in the back (not the lead) so Sucker Punch punishes setup turns; additionally consider swapping Whimsicott's Encore for Helping Hand is wrong — keep Encore but add Taunt consideration on a future variant, since Taunt (not Encore) is the real anti-Coil tool." (Reason: Encore cannot stop a self-boosting foe (it locks them into boosting), but Taunt does. Their entire win condition was a +6 Milotic we had no tool to stop; one Taunt or one focused double-attack turn would have ended it.)
