[2026-10-02 22:24:08 EDT]   [Review] What worked: Won vs 6leen. Turn 1 Heat Wave + Moonblast into Gallade/Scolipede immediately pressured the Trick Room/setup pair and the spread hit Ditto's switch-in for 48%, so Ditto died on T2. Recognizing Wide Guard after T3 and swapping to single-target Weather Ball on T4 to delete the +2 Atk/+3 Spe Scolipede was the key play of the game — exactly the 'after Wide Guard, don't spread into it' rule. The endgame was clean: Whimsicott at 1 HP Protected, Encored Mega Aggron into Iron Defense (denying Body Press/Head Smash entirely), then Moonblast finished it. Encoring a setup move is a textbook win-con denial.
[2026-10-02 22:24:08 EDT]   [Review] What failed: 1) T5 fallback: Drogon fired Heat Wave into a freshly Mega-evolved Aggron that had Wide Guard support AND Head Smash — the spread was blocked by Wide Guard and Drogon was OHKO'd. Two straight turns of Wide Guard were already visible; this was a 100% avoidable loss of our Mega. 2) Kingambit entered against Mega Aggron with Body Press and died in one hit for 0% damage — a second confirmation that Kingambit must not be deployed against Fighting coverage. 3) Garchomp did a miserable 54% total over four turns because Stomping Tantrum into a +4 Def Iron Defense Aggron is near-worthless; the game was won by Whimsicott's Encore, not by damage. 4) Two rate-limit fallbacks again cost us the single most important turn of the game (T5).
[2026-10-02 22:24:08 EDT] [Team Inspiration] Recorded suggestion inspired by opponent build: "Copy their 'Wide Guard pivot + unkillable Mega wall' shape: Gallade's Wide Guard blanked two of our Charizard turns while Mega Aggron set Iron Defense behind it. For us, swap Mega Dragonite's Heat Wave (spread, Wide-Guardable) for Extreme Speed — an unblockable single-target priority finisher that ignores Wide Guard, redirection and paralysis speed loss." (Reason: Across the last five games our spread moves were blocked three times (Wide Guard x2, Protect) and Dragonite/Drogon repeatedly had no way to convert a last turn into damage; a priority single-target move fixes both failure modes at once.)
