[2026-10-02 22:43:41 EDT]   [Review] What worked: Drogon + Whimsicott lead into a Trick Room archetype was correct and decisive: Drought instantly removed any weather plan, Heat Wave spread chip burned Farigiraf on T1 (burn did the heavy lifting — it dropped Farigiraf from 40% to 34% and halved its physical threat), and T2 Heat Wave KO'd Mega Heracross through the Encore lock. Whimsicott's Encore onto Heracross after it clicked Protect on T1 was the textbook play from the playbook: Encore a Protect-happy Mega and it loses every subsequent turn (it immediately failed its forced Protect, eating the full Heat Wave). Identifying Heracross-Mega as the single win condition and focusing both actions at it won the game in 2 turns.
[2026-10-02 22:43:41 EDT]   [Review] What failed: Only one real error: T1 Moonblast into Heracross. Heracross had just Mega-evolved on its first turn facing a Drought Charizard-Y — a Protect read there is near-free, and the current ANTI-PROTECT-BAIT proposal (don't point a second action at a foe likely to Protect) says to redirect. Moonblast into Farigiraf instead would have added ~25-30% plus the burn chip, likely putting Farigiraf in Heat Wave KO range on T2 before it could switch to Skeledirge, and would have given the Trick Room setter real pressure rather than a free TR turn. Secondary: our damage-calc model under-read Heat Wave on Farigiraf (predicted 25-35%, actual 60%), meaning our calc assumptions about opposing bulk in this format remain too generous — we are leaving KOs on the table by assuming foes are bulkier than they are. Also, Farigiraf got Trick Room up uncontested; the game ended fast enough that it didn't matter, but had Skeledirge + Pinsir come in under TR that would have been a live problem.
[2026-10-02 22:43:41 EDT] [Team Inspiration] Recorded suggestion inspired by opponent build: "Their Heracross-Mega + Farigiraf shell shows the value of a Protect-first Mega that stalls a turn while the partner sets the field. For us, keep Protect on Drogon but swap Mega Dragonite's Heat Wave for Extreme Speed: a +2 finisher ignores Protect timing games, Wide Guard and redirection, and gives a chipped Dragonite a usable turn (five straight games where our Mega had no way to convert). Also consider Covert Cloak on Drogon to blank Fake Out/flinch on its Mega turn." (Reason: We won here because Encore removed their Protect cycling; in the losses our Megas lost their turn to Fake Out/flinch or were walled by Protect timing. A priority finisher plus a flinch-blanking item attacks both recurring failure modes directly.)
