[2026-10-02 23:32:42 EDT]   [Review] What worked: Vs Buglife-cn (sand/Rock: Tyrantrum + Glimmora + Hippowdon + Staraptor) we won 6-? by following the anti-sand playbook: turn 1 we correctly read the double-Rock OHKO board and Protected Mega Charizard while pivoting Whimsicott out into Milotic — a textbook Defensive Safe Play (Protect threatened slot + reposition partner). Milotic then became the engine of the game: Ice Beam removed Staraptor, Scald removed Hippowdon (and killed sand), and Whimsicott Moonblast finished Tyrantrum. Milotic Protecting on T5 while Whimsicott cleaned up Tyrantrum was a clean 2-action turn. The T6 switch of 11% poisoned Milotic into Garchomp (resists Rock, threatens Glimmora with Ground) followed exactly the 'a slot facing a displayed OHKO must switch' lesson, and Garchomp's Stomping Tantrum eventually closed out Glimmora — notably Stomping Tantrum was the correct pick over Earthquake because it doubles power through Spiky Shield/Protect cycling and spares Whimsicott.
[2026-10-02 23:32:42 EDT]   [Review] What failed: 1) Drogon died on turn 2 to Tyrantrum Rock Slide while the engine fallback (HTTP 429 rate limit) picked an attack instead of a second Protect or a switch — we knew Rock Slide was a 268-439% OHKO window and had just Protected, so the only correct T2 action was SWITCH Drogon out, not attack. That is a direct violation of 'a slot that Protected last turn and still faces a displayed OHKO must SWITCH'. The rate-limit fallback engine does not enforce our coach lessons, so our most important piece evaporated for zero value. 2) Milotic took a Mortal Spin poison on turn 1 and spent the whole game bleeding; we never used Recover once despite Milotic being our main damage dealer (113% and 2 KOs) — Recover + Leftovers would have kept it alive as the late-game wall and we would not have needed the Garchomp pivot. 3) Whimsicott's turn-7 Tailwind was a wasted action: at 26% HP, poisoned, against a single Glimmora, with Garchomp already outspeeding it; Protect was the only correct move and would have saved the piece. 4) Two full turns (T7, T8) were spent clicking into Glimmora's Spiky Shield/Protect cycle — against a last-mon Protect cycler we should alternate attack/Protect to break the pattern rather than clicking the same move twice.
